1. Introduction to SQL Injection

SQL Injection (SQLi) is a web application attack where an attacker injects malicious SQL queries into an application’s input fields to interact with the backend database.
If the application does not properly validate user input, attackers can read, modify, or delete database data.

SQL Injection is one of the most critical web vulnerabilities and is listed in the OWASP Top 10.

2. Why SQL Injection Is Dangerous

SQL Injection can allow attackers to:

Bypass authentication

Access sensitive data

Modify database records

Delete databases

Execute administrative operations

3. How SQL Injection Works

A vulnerable application directly inserts user input into an SQL query.

Example Vulnerable Query
SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = '$user' AND password = '$pass';


If input is not sanitized, an attacker can manipulate the query logic.

4. Types of SQL Injection
4.1 In-Band SQL Injection

Uses the same channel to inject and retrieve data.

a) Error-Based SQL Injection

Uses database error messages

Reveals database structure

b) Union-Based SQL Injection

Uses UNION SELECT

Extracts data from other tables

4.2 Blind SQL Injection

No error messages shown, but behavior changes.

a) Boolean-Based

True/false conditions

Observes page response

b) Time-Based

Uses delays (SLEEP)

Measures server response time

4.3 Out-of-Band SQL Injection

Uses external channels (DNS, HTTP)

Rare but powerful

5. Common SQL Injection Points

Login forms

Search fields

URL parameters

Cookies

HTTP headers

6. SQL Injection Authentication Bypass
Example Payload
' OR '1'='1' --


This makes the condition always true, allowing login without valid credentials.

